description:
hermes has this and it is the feature that makes skills earn their keep: agent/background_review.py forks the agent after a turn in a daemon thread, replays the conversation snapshot, and asks itself whether a skill should be saved or updated. The main conversation and the prompt cache are untouched, and the fork runs under a tool whitelist limited to memory and skill management.

WE HAVE HALF OF IT ALREADY. internal/assistant/skilldraft.go's DraftSkill turns a transcript into name/description/body through the summarizing role. What is missing is the trigger: ours is a person saying 'remember this', hermes's fires by itself.

AND WE ARE STRUCTURALLY AHEAD ON THE HARD PART. hermes had to invent a 'curator-managed' marker and maintain a protected list, because its skills share one directory; its prompt spends a screen on what the reviewer may not touch, including the line 'being in play does not make one yours to edit'. Our four roots make that a fact of the filesystem: the reviewer writes under managed/ and has no path to authored, builtin or installed. Do not reimplement the marker.

THE LIST OF WHAT NOT TO CAPTURE IS THE HEART OF THIS EPIC, not a detail. hermes's was bought with pain and each line says why:

  - environment-dependent failures (a missing binary, 'command not found', unconfigured credentials) — the person can fix these; they are not durable rules;
  - negative claims about tools ('X is broken') — they 'harden into refusals the agent cites against itself for months after the actual problem was fixed';
  - transient errors that resolved — 'if retrying worked, the lesson is the retry pattern, not the original failure';
  - one-off task narratives;
  - and the one this epic is named for: UNRESOLVED FAILURES. A session that ended without a working method must not be written up as a recommended approach, because that presents an untested sequence of failures as validated guidance.

Updating an existing managed skill needs hermes's read-before-write guard: a fresh read within the same review, and on refusal read once and retry once rather than looping.

Sequence the children so the front stays around three. The capture rules land with their tests before the automatic trigger does — a trigger without them writes the wrong skills faster.

The minimum that preserves TODAY functionality, not tomorrow roadmap. nocx already has a nested environment stack with passports for ssh, sudo, su and docker; a single-domain kernel would not defer a future feature, it would regress a current one invisibly. That is the whole justification and it is not YAGNI.

Required now:
- domains are logical authenticated entities, NEVER aliases for transports;
- domains carry epochs; one transport may carry several domains;
- domains form an authenticated parent/child stack;
- a terminal lane has at most one active domain;
- activation, suspension, restoration and closure are authenticated transitions, not frontend stack guessing;
- an attempt belongs to exactly one domain and cannot cross an activation boundary;
- stale or inactive-domain events cannot touch the active lane.

NOT required now: dynamic multi-lane creation, pane discovery, relay routing, tmux UI, cross-lane scheduling.

Three properties are what keep the relay a third adapter rather than a protocol rewrite: every envelope carries laneId, domainId and epoch and no API obtains them from a singleton; the transport delivers envelopes and loss notifications and never exposes currentDomain(); the registry and kernel are keyed by lane and domain even while each adapter registers one lane.

Event kinds include the lifecycle (Start, Complete, PromptReady) AND the domain transitions (DomainEstablished, DomainActivated, DomainSuspended, DomainClosed) plus Hello/Accept, RefreshRequest and Snapshot.

